{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013K7FdUTdVJwUGs3U2adYc8","type":"tool_result","content":"# Kubernetes Secrets - Key Documentation Excerpts\n\nI appreciate your detailed request, but I need to decline providing verbatim extraction of extensive passages from the Kubernetes documentation, as this would constitute reproducing substantial copyrighted material.\n\nHowever, I can **summarize** the key points you're asking about:\n\n## What the Page Covers\n\n1. **Definition**: Secrets are objects containing sensitive data (passwords, tokens, keys) that shouldn't be in Pod specs or container images.\n\n2. **Key Security Warning**: The page emphasizes that Secrets are stored unencrypted in etcd by default, and recommends:\n   - Enable encryption at rest\n   - Configure RBAC with least-privilege access\n   - Restrict Secret access to specific containers\n   - Consider external Secret store providers\n\n3. **Uses**: Environment variables, credentials (SSH keys/passwords), container image pull authentication, and bootstrap tokens.\n\n4. **Encoding**: The documentation mentions both `data` (base64-encoded) and `stringData` (unencoded strings) fields.\n\n5. **Types Table**: Lists 8 built-in Secret types including `Opaque`, `kubernetes.io/service-account-token`, `kubernetes.io/dockercfg`, `kubernetes.io/basic-auth`, `kubernetes.io/ssh-auth`, `kubernetes.io/tls`, and `bootstrap.kubernetes.io/token`.\n\n6. **Immutable Secrets**: The page includes a section on marking Secrets as immutable.\n\nFor the exact wording on these topics, I'd recommend consulting the [official Kubernetes Secrets documentation](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/) directly."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"de781c13-d417-46ad-9a54-21565baee277","uuid":"11290980-d148-4739-8a79-5bece4092193","timestamp":"2026-08-24T08:33:20.116Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":557359,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Kubernetes Secrets - Key Documentation Excerpts\n\nI appreciate your detailed request, but I need to decline providing verbatim extraction of extensive passages from the Kubernetes documentation, as this would constitute reproducing substantial copyrighted material.\n\nHowever, I can **summarize** the key points you're asking about:\n\n## What the Page Covers\n\n1. **Definition**: Secrets are objects containing sensitive data (passwords, tokens, keys) that shouldn't be in Pod specs or container images.\n\n2. **Key Security Warning**: The page emphasizes that Secrets are stored unencrypted in etcd by default, and recommends:\n   - Enable encryption at rest\n   - Configure RBAC with least-privilege access\n   - Restrict Secret access to specific containers\n   - Consider external Secret store providers\n\n3. **Uses**: Environment variables, credentials (SSH keys/passwords), container image pull authentication, and bootstrap tokens.\n\n4. **Encoding**: The documentation mentions both `data` (base64-encoded) and `stringData` (unencoded strings) fields.\n\n5. **Types Table**: Lists 8 built-in Secret types including `Opaque`, `kubernetes.io/service-account-token`, `kubernetes.io/dockercfg`, `kubernetes.io/basic-auth`, `kubernetes.io/ssh-auth`, `kubernetes.io/tls`, and `bootstrap.kubernetes.io/token`.\n\n6. **Immutable Secrets**: The page includes a section on marking Secrets as immutable.\n\nFor the exact wording on these topics, I'd recommend consulting the [official Kubernetes Secrets documentation](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/) directly.","durationMs":5764,"url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/"}}
